---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'EB-5 for OPT and STEM OPT Students | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for OPT and STEM OPT students explains how concurrent filing can interact with your work authorization window before relying on H-1B lottery outcomes.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-for-opt-students`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 for OPT and STEM OPT Students';
const subhead = 'How OPT and STEM OPT students can evaluate EB-5 during their work authorization window.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'F-1 Student to EB-5 Self-Sponsored Green Card',
    url: '/pathways/f1-to-eb5-self-sponsored-green-card',
    description: 'Full student pathway overview from F-1 through concurrent filing.',
  },
  {
    title: 'STEM OPT EB-5 for Indian and Chinese Students — Cap-Gap Strategy',
    url: '/research/f1-students/stem-opt-eb5-indian-chinese',
    description: 'STEM OPT timing, cap-gap risk, and EB-5 as a parallel planning track.',
  },
];
---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About Your OPT Timeline and EB-5"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for OPT Students"
  keywords={['EB-5 for OPT students', 'STEM OPT EB-5', 'OPT concurrent filing', 'EB-5 during STEM OPT']}
>
  <h2>Why some students evaluate EB-5 during OPT or STEM OPT</h2>
  <p>
    OPT and STEM OPT provide temporary work authorization after graduation, not a permanent immigration path. Many
    students use that window to pursue H-1B selection — but lottery outcomes are uncertain, and STEM OPT still ends on a
    fixed date. Some students and families therefore evaluate EB-5 while work authorization is still active, so source-of-funds
    documentation and project selection are not rushed into the final months of OPT. This is a planning choice, not a
    guaranteed outcome: EB-5 has its own eligibility, capital, and adjudication requirements.
  </p>

  <h2>How concurrent filing can interact with an active OPT timeline</h2>
  <p>
    For eligible investors already in the United States, concurrent filing can allow Form I-526E and Form I-485 to be
    submitted together when a visa number is available in the relevant category. In that scenario, some applicants also
    seek an Employment Authorization Document (EAD) and Advance Parole while the petition is pending. Whether that
    timing helps your specific OPT or STEM OPT calendar depends on filing readiness, category availability, and attorney
    guidance — it is not automatic simply because you hold OPT. For the broader student pathway framing, see
    <a href="/pathways/f1-to-eb5-self-sponsored-green-card">F-1 to EB-5 self-sponsored green card</a>. Country-specific
    STEM OPT timing risks are covered in
    <a href="/research/f1-students/stem-opt-eb5-indian-chinese">STEM OPT EB-5 for Indian and Chinese students</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What to prepare before OPT ends</h2>
  <p>
    Students who consider EB-5 during OPT typically focus on documenting lawful source and path of funds, selecting a
    project with clear job-creation methodology, and confirming current program deadlines and investment thresholds before
    authorization expires. Full step-by-step mechanics live on the
    <a href="/eb5-investment-process">EB-5 investment process</a> page — use that guide for filing sequence detail rather
    than treating OPT as a substitute for petition readiness.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
